{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>forms := form.NewTranslatedForm(func(loc form.Localizer, key string, args ...any) string {
    return tr.Tl(loc, key, args...)
})
forms.SetTheme("plain")
errs := forms.ValidateFormLocalized(&amp;input, loc)</code></pre>
<p>Here <code>tr</code> is a loaded go-translator instance and <code>loc</code> implements <code>GetLocale() string</code>. Load the PO languages before configuring the renderer; do not set a global current locale during a request. <code>Bind</code> takes the localizer too.</p>
<pre><code>{{"{{"}} form_render_localized .Loc .Form .Errors }}</code></pre>
<h2>Catalogue keys</h2>
<pre><code>msgid "Name"
msgstr "Naam"

msgid "Save contact"
msgstr "Contact opslaan"

msgid "form||Validation required"
msgstr "Dit veld is verplicht."

msgid "form||Value should not exceed %d characters"
msgstr "Gebruik maximaal %d tekens."</code></pre>
<p>The callback receives labels, placeholders, descriptions, submit/cancel text, the messages per rule and go-form’s own keys: the validation messages (<code>form.TranslationKey…</code>), the screen-reader text of required fields (<code>form||(required)</code>) and the default cancel text (<code>Cancel</code>). Keep the exact built-in key and preserve its formatting placeholders.</p>
<h2>go-form’s own texts</h2>
<p>When nothing translates one of go-form’s messages, it shows in the language of the localizer when go-form has a catalogue for it, and in English otherwise: <code>form||Validation required</code> shows as <code>Dit veld is verplicht.</code> for <code>nl</code>, <code>nl_NL</code> or <code>nl-BE</code>, and as <code>This field is required.</code> for a language go-form has no catalogue for. Nothing translates a message when there is no callback, or when the callback returns it as it got it, with or without <code>form||</code>. A translation of your own wins, unless it equals the key without <code>form||</code>.</p>
<p>go-translator shows a missing message as <code>*key*</code> by default. Pass <code>translator.WithUntranslated(translator.ShowOriginal)</code> to <code>translator.New</code>, or call <code>tr.SetUntranslated(translator.ShowOriginal)</code>, so that it returns such a message as written and go-form’s texts show.</p>
<p>For another language, translate go-form’s messages in your own catalogues: <code>locales/translations.pot</code> in the go-form repository lists them.</p>
<h2>Choices and custom messages</h2>
<p>Labels from a <code>values</code> tag are translated; add <code>translate:"false"</code> to show them as written. Labels from an <code>Enumerator</code>, <code>Mapper</code> or <code>SortedMapper</code>, such as a <code>SortedSelect</code>, are often data and show as written; use <code>translate:"true"</code> when they are translation keys. An <code>Enumerator</code> then uses keys such as <code>enum||Status.draft</code>, and <code>form.DefaultEnumTranslation = true</code> turns it on for all such fields that have no <code>translate</code> tag. Messages per rule (<code>requiredMsg</code>, <code>patternMsg</code>, …) are translated like labels. <code>errorMsg</code> and the messages of <code>RegisterValidationMethod</code> validators show as written; <code>RegisterLocalizedValidation</code> validators get the localizer and their messages are translated.</p>
<h2>Extracting texts</h2>
<pre><code>texts := form.Texts(Contact{}, form.WithInfo(SignupInput{}, info), contactSchema)
catalog.Add(extract.Texts("go:forms", texts...)...)</code></pre>
<p>Template key extraction does not scan struct tags. <code>form.Texts(models...)</code> returns the texts of forms, each once, in order of appearance: labels, legends, placeholders, descriptions, option labels that are translated, submit and cancel texts, and every tag ending in <code>Msg</code>. Add them to the POT file, for example with go-translator’s <code>extract</code> package. go-form’s own messages are in its catalogues. Use the same localizer for validation and redisplay so a request does not mix languages.</p>
</article>
